            <ArticleTitle>Treatment options in painful diabetic neuropathy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>36-42; discussion 48-52</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Diabetic neuropathy is common in patients with diabetes mellitus, and 7.5% of diabetics experience pain from diabetic neuropathy. Complications of diabetes mellitus are more common where control of the disease is not optimal. By improving the control of the disease, both the neuropathy and the pain it can produce may be improved. The pain of diabetic neuropathy can frequently be controlled using analgesics, antidepressants, anticonvulsants, topical capsaicin, and neuromodulation, either alone or in any combination.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
